WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.059
Only Alexander the Great could tame the horse.

00:00:03.059 --> 00:00:07.299
A Transformer spends layers working out that those three tokens are one name.

00:00:07.679 --> 00:00:09.349
What if it could just look the phrase up?

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:13.923
Hi, I'm Solveig!

00:00:13.923 --> 00:00:18.419
This is conditional memory, and two of this year's largest open models ship it.

00:00:21.183 --> 00:00:22.891
Take the last two or three tokens.

00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:24.654
Hash their ids.

00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:29.778
Each head reduces the hash modulo its own prime, and picks one

00:00:29.778 --> 00:00:31.192
row of its own table.

00:00:31.592 --> 00:00:32.728
Glue the rows together.

00:00:34.192 --> 00:00:37.258
The hidden state gates them, and they join the residual stream.

00:00:39.258 --> 00:00:43.020
Under one prime, two unrelated phrases can land on the same row.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.358
Under another prime, they almost never collide as well.

00:00:46.358 --> 00:00:48.627
The rows together still tell them apart.

00:00:50.358 --> 00:00:52.356
A fetched row knows nothing about context.

00:00:52.356 --> 00:00:53.783
So the model checks it.

00:00:54.203 --> 00:00:55.856
The hidden state acts as a query.

00:00:57.639 --> 00:00:58.981
The memory becomes a key.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.018
If they disagree, the gate closes, and a collision stays out.

00:01:05.558 --> 00:01:06.923
Here is how big this gets.

00:01:08.558 --> 00:01:11.203
One table holds over a quarter of the parameters.

00:01:11.203 --> 00:01:13.261
A token reads sixteen rows of it.

00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:18.642
The addresses are known before the layer runs, so rows can be

00:01:18.642 --> 00:01:19.979
prefetched from the CPU.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.652
A hundred billion parameters cost under three percent.

00:01:24.158 --> 00:01:27.823
Split a fixed budget between experts and memory, and the best loss

00:01:27.823 --> 00:01:29.045
is not all experts.

00:01:29.045 --> 00:01:31.793
About a fifth to a quarter goes to memory.

00:01:32.173 --> 00:01:35.610
And a bigger table keeps lowering the loss, at no extra compute.

00:01:37.183 --> 00:01:39.329
Some parameters only need to be looked up.

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:41.744
And a lookup can live far from the GPU.

00:01:45.358 --> 00:01:46.362
Hash, fetch, gate.

00:01:46.362 --> 00:01:47.367
Thanks for watching!

00:01:49.958 --> 00:01:52.137
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:52.137 --> 00:01:52.760
I'm Solveig.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:53.071
Bye!
